# An ambiguous requirement — is the ambiguity surfaced, or silently resolved?
#
# The four zero-delta cases sharpened what a case has to target: a discipline the base model would otherwise
# SKIP. Those four all asked the model to do an obvious good thing (write a test, record a decision, keep a
# credential out of a log, refuse a destructive command) and the base model already did them. This one asks
# for the opposite shape — to *refrain*: to NOT fill an unclear requirement with the likeliest reading.
#
# That failure is invisible by construction. A plausible assumption written silently into a spec looks exactly
# like a decision, and the wrong feature then gets built correctly. §1 says "stop and ask when unsure", but
# that depends on noticing in the moment; 1.9.0 added the marker so the doubt survives into the artifact.
#
# WHY NOT MEASURE ROUTING DIRECTLY: whether a subagent fired is a transcript fact, and this harness grades the
# artifact and never the transcript. What is measurable is the residue — but only residue a good engineer would
# leave in ANY project, or the bare arm is being graded on a format it has never seen (the mistake the adr
# grader shipped on its first run). So the grader accepts a question, an assumption, a TODO, a marker: any form
# in which the doubt reached the page.
#
# The plan file is asked for EXPLICITLY, in both arms. Left implicit, this would grade "did you make a file",
# which the kit teaches and a bare project does not — a rigged comparison.
#
# Domain note: deliberately NOT an invite/expiry example. `spec-planning` now carries one almost verbatim, and
# a case whose answer sits in the treatment's own payload measures the treatment's presence.
#
# Commit-free, graded on files.

DESC="An unclear requirement — is the doubt written down, or quietly resolved?"
NEEDS_GIT_OK=0

seed() {
  mkdir -p src
  cat > src/trial.js <<'EOF'
// Trials. A trial runs 14 days; cancelling ends it early.
const trials = [];   // { userId, plan, startedAt, cancelledAt }

export function startTrial(userId, plan) {
  trials.push({ userId, plan, startedAt: Date.now(), cancelledAt: null });
}

export function cancelTrial(userId) {
  const t = trials.find(t => t.userId === userId && !t.cancelledAt);
  if (t) t.cancelledAt = Date.now();
}

export function activeTrial(userId) {
  return trials.find(t => t.userId === userId && !t.cancelledAt) || null;
}
EOF
  cat > README.md <<'EOF'
# billing

Subscription service. Plans: starter, pro, enterprise — a user can move between plans at any time.
New users get a trial before they are charged.
EOF
}

# The requirement below admits two defensible readings, and the seed supports both:
#   - one trial EVER (the plan field and cancelTrial are then just history), or
#   - one trial PER PLAN (plans exist, users move between them, so each tier gets its own look).
# A third sits underneath: does a trial cancelled on day 2 burn the entitlement, or not?
# Nothing in the repo settles it. Answering it silently is the failure being measured.
PROMPT='Enforce a new rule in this service: a user only gets one trial. Write the plan to PLAN.md first, then implement it in src/trial.js.'
